---
pagination:
  data: collections.allChapters
  size: 1
  alias: chapter
permalink: "/c/{{ chapter.data.comment_id }}/"
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#-
  PERMANENT CITATION ALIASES. One redirect stub per chapter at
  /c/<comment_id>/, pointing at wherever that chapter currently lives.

  THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. A chapter's URL is not permanent. Episodes get
  renumbered in place to keep story chronology in order, which reassigns a URL
  like /seasons/s00/e02/s00e02c01/ to entirely different content - and it has
  already happened once, when a new chapter took over S00E02C01's old slot. An
  external wiki, blog or citation pointing at that URL does not 404 afterwards;
  it silently starts pointing at something else, which is strictly worse,
  because nothing signals the breakage to either side.

  WHY comment_id IS THE RIGHT KEY, AND WHY NOTHING NEW HAD TO BE INVENTED. The
  repo already solved this problem once, for giscus discussions, and the answer
  is sitting in every chapter's front matter. `comment_id` is required on every
  chapter (lib/content-schema.js), permanent by policy - never regenerated, even
  when a chapter is renumbered - carried forward by hand with the CONTENT rather
  than the slot, and unique by build-time check: scripts/validate-content.js
  fails the build on any collision. That is precisely the set of properties a
  citable identifier needs. All 35 are also plain lowercase slugs, so they need
  no escaping in a URL.

  WHY STATIC STUBS RATHER THAN SERVER REDIRECTS. An .htaccess rewrite would be
  tidier on cPanel and does not exist at all on GitHub Pages, and this repo
  deploys to both. A generated HTML stub works identically everywhere and needs
  no host configuration. Same pattern, and the same head, as the two existing
  rename stubs (src/glossary/dynarch.njk, src/lore/solar-system-concordant.njk).

  WHY noindex + canonical HERE, when base.njk deliberately avoids that pairing
  for excluded placeholders: for a REDIRECT the two agree rather than conflict.
  canonical names the real page, noindex keeps the alias itself out of the index
  so it never competes with the chapter for the same content. That is the
  conventional treatment of a redirect stub, and the opposite of a placeholder,
  which has no canonical target worth naming.

  SCENE-POV PAGES GET NO ALIAS, deliberately. Their comment_ids are derived
  (<chapter>-scene-N-<character>) and they are derivative views of a chapter
  rather than the citable unit; ~92 more stubs would earn nothing. A citation
  wanting a specific POV can cite the chapter alias and name the scene.

  Built from the UNFILTERED allChapters collection (see .eleventy.js), so the
  alias resolves on every domain - including one that hides this chapter, where
  it lands on the excluded.njk placeholder that points onward to a domain which
  has it. An alias that disappeared on the narrowing clones would break the very
  links it exists to preserve.
-#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ chapter.data.title }} — permanent link</title>
<link rel="canonical" href="/star-rangers{{ chapter.url }}">
<meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">
<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url=/star-rangers{{ chapter.url }}">
<style>
  :root { color-scheme: dark light; }
  body {
    margin: 0; min-height: 100vh; display: grid; place-items: center;
    padding: 2rem; background: #12151b; color: #e8e6e1;
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif; line-height: 1.6;
  }
  main { max-width: 34rem; text-align: center; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: normal; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  a { color: #9db8d8; }
  .note { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.7; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <h1>{{ chapter.data.title }}</h1>
  <p>This is the permanent citation link for
    <strong>S{{ chapter.data.season | zeroPad }}E{{ chapter.data.episode | zeroPad }}C{{ chapter.data.chapter | zeroPad }} — {{ chapter.data.title }}</strong>.
    It stays valid even if the chapter is renumbered, so it is the address to
    quote in a wiki, a citation, or anywhere else you need a link that will not
    quietly come to mean something else.</p>
  <p><a href="/star-rangers{{ chapter.url }}">Continue to the chapter</a></p>
  <p class="note">If you are not redirected automatically, follow the link above.</p>
</main>
</body>
</html>
